{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And it shall be when your son asks you tomorrow, saying: What is this?</b> The author of the Haggadah said that this verse speaks of the simple son (תָּם), and Maharil, in his work Zevach Pesach, raised the difficulty: from where do we know that this speaks of the simple son? Perhaps he says 'What is this?' by way of mockery, like the wicked son? And he answered that we learn it from the answer that HaShem commanded to give him: 'By strength of hand HaShem brought us out of Egypt' (Exodus 13:14) — since HaShem commanded to answer him fittingly, surely he asked fittingly. But this answer is not fitting, and all who see it will say 'What is this?' — for this is an obvious matter: that everyone who says 'What is this' is a תָּם. And if before Him, blessed be He, his intent was revealed, before us who is it revealed? And how shall I know what is in the heart of the questioner? Also, the Torah does not declare future events such as these." "And it seems closer to me to say the following: that it was difficult for the author of the Haggadah why, regarding two sons, the expression 'tomorrow' (מָחָר) was mentioned — once in this verse, and a second time in parashat Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 6:20): 'When your son asks you tomorrow, saying.' But regarding the other two sons the expression 'tomorrow' was not mentioned; on the contrary, it says 'And you shall tell your son on that day' — literally, and that is the opposite of 'tomorrow.' And it is difficult: why was the expression 'tomorrow' not mentioned for all of them, or 'on that day' for all of them? And further it is difficult: why did the Torah mention the question 'What is this?' in connection with the redemption of the firstborn, and why did it not mention this question regarding pesach, matzah, and maror, as it mentioned it for the wicked son and for the one who does not know how to ask?" "From the force of these difficulties, the author of the Haggadah saw fit to say that this verse speaks of the תָּם, and that in parashat Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 6:20) it speaks of the wise son (חָכָם), and the 'tomorrow' (מָחָר) stated regarding both of them is not a tomorrow after some time but literally tomorrow. For the תָּם — all the while that his father gives him pesach, matzah, and maror, he asks nothing at all, for surely he does not mock the service and the commandments of HaShem. And a reason for the commandment he also does not seek, for would that they fed him all day long choice roasted cuts and fine-flour matzot on bitter herbs — whose way is to whet the appetite — so why should the תָּם seek a reason for all these, when he lacks nothing for himself? But regarding the redemption of the son, where they give him nothing at all but rather demand of him that he give the redemption money — then he asks 'What is this?' For even a תָּם and a complete simpleton is nevertheless wise in this matter, to hold on to his money so as not to give money to any person for nothing. However, from the word מָחָר we learn that he is a תָּם and upright, and does not mock the giving and does not refuse it. For on that very day when they demand of him the redemption money he says nothing at all, and gives his money, and does not seek to know the reason for the matter, so as not to be suspected of not giving with good will — and so that they should not say he is picking a fight against the commandments of HaShem, saying 'Why should I give my money for nothing?' Therefore he keeps silent on the day of giving and gives his money to fulfill the commandment of HaShem. Nevertheless, on the next day (מָחֳרָת) he asks 'What is this?', for he desires to know the reason why HaShem commanded us to give the redemption money of the firstborn." "And this sign the Torah handed down to us: that if he asks you tomorrow 'What is this?' — on the day after the day of giving, while on the day of giving he asks nothing at all — then surely with a whole heart (תָּם לְבָבוֹ) and with clean hands he asks 'What is this.' For had he been a scoffer or one who picks a fight against the commandments of HaShem, he would have said his words on the day of giving in order to nullify the giving. And since he kept silent on the day of giving and asks only on the next day, he is surely תָּם and upright and asks fittingly. Therefore you answer him fittingly: 'For by strength of hand HaShem brought us out of Egypt, and HaShem slew all the firstborn,' etc. Or if the son sees his father giving the redemption of firstborn animals and asks him nothing at all on the day of giving — lest he be suspected of intending to prevent his father from the commandment — and asks him on the next day, he too is counted a תָּם. And since he asks in brief, 'What is this?', and lacks the understanding to detail all the matters, from this it is judged that he is a תָּם and is not sharp enough to examine the details of the matter. But had he asked in this manner on the day of giving itself, it could be that he is not a תָּם." "And likewise the verse 'When your son asks you tomorrow' (מָחָר), stated in parashat Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 6:20), which is expounded regarding the question of the wise son — there too it used 'tomorrow' for the day after the day of the service. For the wise son fears to ask on the day of the performance of the commandment, lest they suspect him — hearing him — of mocking the commandments at the time of their performance, in order to stop the people from the labor of the service of HaShem, blessed be He. For otherwise, many raise the difficulty: what is the difference between the words of the wicked son and the words of the wise son? And not many are wise enough to draw, from the precision of the language, a distinction between them — and perhaps they would suspect him of being a scoffer. And to escape this suspicion, he too asks on the next day after the performance. But the wicked son, whose entire intention is to stop the people from the labor of the service of HaShem, blessed be He — therefore he says his words at the time of the performance of the commandment and mocks them, saying: 'What are you busy with? Elders like you should busy themselves with these idle things?' Therefore the expression 'tomorrow' (מָחָר) was not mentioned in his words. And therefore the Torah said, 'And you shall tell your son on that day' (וּבַיּוֹם הַהוּא) — just as he says his words on that day of the performance, so you too shall tell him your words immediately. For we have already said that in this verse there is a telling (הַגָּדָה) for two sons. If for the wicked son — it is obvious that you must answer him immediately, lest he grow wise in his own eyes and say that there is no answer in your mouth, and so you pushed him off with a reed, saying: Go and come back tomorrow, I will give you an answer. Therefore 'Answer a fool according to his folly' (Proverbs 26:5) — immediately, on that very day. And if for the son who does not know how to ask — why should you tell him your words tomorrow? Is it not more correct to tell him your words at the hour when there is matzah and maror lying before you, in order to draw his heart to the service of HaShem?" "And along the way I shall also explain the words of the Wise Son spoken in the portion Va'etchanan, for one may raise a difficulty against the passage in the Haggadah that says, 'And you too shall say to him: according to the laws of the pesach, they do not conclude after the pesach with afikoman' — why did it not mention the answer written alongside it in the portion Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 6:21)? And I say that the Wise Son asks for the reason of pesach, matzah, and maror in the order in which they are written in the Torah, as it says (Exodus 12:8), 'And they shall eat the flesh — roast with fire — and unleavened bread upon bitter herbs,' and concerning these three he asks and says, 'What are the testimonies and the statutes and the judgments.' Concerning the 'roast with fire' of the pesach he says 'what are the testimonies,' for the slaughter of the pesach gives a trustworthy testimony to YHWH that the Holy One, blessed be He, rules over the array of forces, even over the constellation of Aries, which is the firstborn and head of all the constellations — and all the more so over the other constellations, as it is written, 'And the blood shall be to you for a sign' — for every sign (אוֹת) is a testimony (עֵדוּת) and a proof concerning some matter, as explained above regarding the verse 'This month shall be to you' (Exodus 12:2). 'And the statutes' — this is the matzah, whose essence is lowliness, like this dough that does not rise upward, as all this is explained at length in my small work 'Orach le-Chayyim,' discourse 25, for every statute (חֹק) indicates something that has a law and a boundary, as written concerning the sea (Job 38:10), 'And I broke a statute for it,' and it says, 'Thus far shall you come and no further.' So too a law and boundary were given to the dough of the matzah that it not rise, for from it we take instruction concerning lowliness. And therefore the matzah is a sign of freedom, for in the merit of lowliness our fathers were redeemed from Egypt, as it is written (Deuteronomy 7:7), 'Not because you are more numerous than all the peoples did YHWH set His desire upon you, but because you are the fewest' — in the merit that you diminish yourselves before Me. And therefore the law of leaven is burning, for whoever exalts himself is judged by fire, as found in the Yalkut (portion Tzav, 652) on the verse, 'It is the burnt-offering on the hearth' — for the nature of fire is to rise upward; therefore one who rises upward is judged by it. And the law of leaven is 'any amount' (בְּמַשֶׁהוּ), for such is the measure regarding haughtiness of spirit: in all traits a person must walk the middle path, except for haughtiness of spirit, concerning which our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 5a), 'Not a small amount of it, nor even a minimal amount of it' — behold, they forbade even the slightest. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avot 4:4), 'Exceedingly, exceedingly be of lowly spirit' — for the intensification of the matter and to remove it from all and everything. 'And the judgments' hints at 'And they embittered their lives,' for what was done as judgments (שְׁפָטִים) in Egypt was because they embittered the lives of our fathers, and suffering is called judgment (מִשְׁפָּט), as it says (Isaiah 28:26), 'And he instructs him in judgment.'" "And after the Torah mentioned the question of the Wise Son concerning pesach, matzah, and maror, it said that you shall reverse the order for him and answer him first the reason for the maror, and afterward for the matzah, and afterward for the pesach — so that the Wise Son may learn the order of the acquisition of perfection. And this is its order: for first a person must bring his matter in the crucible of poverty to refine it and purge it of its rust, and through this he will come to lowliness before his Creator, and through lowliness he will come to recognition of the existence of HaShem, blessed be He. For therefore YHWH brought Israel into the crucible of the poverty of Egypt in order to scour their material and bring them to lowliness, and from within it they would come to recognition of the existence of HaShem, blessed be He. And these two that precede are like a preparation to arrive at the third matter, which is knowledge of the existence of HaShem, blessed be He — for this is the desired thing and the ultimate purpose intended from all of them, to bring them to faith in Him, blessed be He, as it is written (Exodus 12:21), 'Draw out and take for yourselves a lamb' — draw your hands from idolatry and take for yourselves a lamb for the worship of HaShem, blessed be He. This is what it says, 'You too shall say to him' — that is to say: even though in the performance of the mitzvah the pesach is mentioned first because it is the ultimate purpose and first in rank, nevertheless you shall answer him about the pesach last, in the order of the answer that is written there, as will be explained." "And do not err to say that the Torah reversed the order of the answer in the manner of 'where one left off, there one begins' (בְּמַאי דִּסְלִיק פָּתַח) — rather, with full intention the Torah reversed the order and said that you shall answer him first concerning the maror, and afterward concerning the matzah, and the pesach at the end, in the order of the acquisition of perfection, so that he may learn from this that the pesach which comes last is the essence. For on this account there is the penalty of excision (כָּרֵת) for the negation of the pesach, which does not apply to matzah and maror, for even one who does not eat matzah and maror is not liable to excision. And the author of the Haggadah was commanded to bring him a sound and good proof from the laws of the pesach that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Pesachim 119b), 'They do not conclude after the pesach with afikoman' — and the reason is so that the taste of the pesach should remain in his mouth last. And why did they not insist that the taste of matzah or maror remain in his mouth last? Rather, because the pesach is the essence and the ultimate end of what is desired from the eating of matzah and maror — that is, because the pesach is a proof concerning faith in HaShem, blessed be He." "And this is what it says, 'You too shall say to him' — that is, just as the Torah reversed the order, so shall you too answer him, and this is the force of 'too' (אַף). And if your son asks you why the Torah reversed the order, you shall bring him proof from the laws of the pesach that our Rabbis of blessed memory said, 'They do not conclude after the pesach with afikoman' — and from here you bring him proof that just as the taste of the pesach is worthy to remain in his mouth last, so too in the answer to the questioning son it must be last, in the order in which it is stated in the Torah. And his intention is to answer him with all the verses stated in the Torah concerning the question of the Wise Son, and this is their order: First it is stated there (Deuteronomy 6:21), 'And you shall say to your son: We were slaves,' and so forth, until 'and YHWH commanded us' — for all this is the reason for the maror, which comes as a remembrance of the bondage, as it says (Exodus 1:14), 'And they embittered their lives with hard labor.' And afterward it says (Deuteronomy 6:24), 'And YHWH commanded us to perform all these statutes,' and so forth, until 'and it shall be righteousness for us.' And all this is the reason for the matzah, which is called a statute (חֹק) as explained above, which instructs concerning lowliness and humility, as it is written (Proverbs 22:4), 'After humility comes fear of YHWH,' and so it is stated here, 'to perform all these statutes, to fear YHWH' — for lowliness brings one to fear. And afterward it says, 'and it shall be righteousness for us,' until the end of the portion — all of it is the answer concerning the pesach, which comes as testimony concerning His governance, blessed be He, for concerning the mitzvah of the pesach it is stated (Exodus 12:13), 'And I will pass over you' — Rashi explained, 'and I will have compassion,' meaning: now you will depart from the domain of the constellations, all of whose actions are necessary and natural, and they have no power to produce any effect by way of compassion or mercy or righteousness, and you will enter under the wings of the Shekhinah, whose action, blessed be He, is through absolute will, bringing deliverances and performing acts of righteousness by way of compassion. And this is what it says, 'And it shall be righteousness for us when we keep to perform all this commandment.' For many commandments depend on the mitzvah of the pesach; therefore it says 'all the commandment' — meaning, a particular commandment upon which many commandments depend — 'before YHWH our Elohim,' meaning: through this commandment we shall depart from under the hand of the array of forces and stand before YHWH our Elohim to enter under the shelter of His wings. And this is the intention of the pesach, and the one who understands shall add a lesson."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {5 "The Wise Son's question is from Deuteronomy 6:20; the Haggadah passage cited is the standard response to the Wise Son. The Yalkut reference to portion Tzav remaz 652 follows the common enumeration.", 6 "The phrase 'כור עני' (crucible of poverty) plays on 'כור' as both a crucible for refining metal and the furnace of affliction; translated literally to preserve both resonances.", 7 "The Aramaic phrase 'במאי דסליק פתח' is a known hermeneutical principle; translated literally with the idiom preserved.", 8 "The citation from Pesachim 119b follows the standard pagination; Rashi on Exodus 12:13 glosses 'ופסחתי' as 'וחמלתי' (and I will have compassion/spare)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:39.501634338Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}